      "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nFollow-up to #60825. `get_processor` now renames the processor's tokenizer to the requested repo. The renderer passes its own tokenizer into the multimodal processor, so for a multimodal model with `--tokenizer` different from `--model`, the renderer's tokenizer gets the model's name and chat templates are resolved from the model repo instead of the tokenizer repo.\n\n## Claims\n\n- Multimodal model with `--tokenizer` != `--model`: chat prompts use the tokenizer repo's template again, as before #60825.\n- Default case (`--tokenizer` = `--model`): unchanged. The renderer's tokenizer still reports the Hub ID and uses the same template.\n\n## Validation\n\nRepro: `LLM(model=\"Qwen/Qwen3-VL-2B-Instruct\", tokenizer=\"Qwen/Qwen3-VL-2B-Thinking\")`, then `llm.chat([{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is 2 + 3?\"}])`. I compare `output.prompt` with each repo's own `AutoTokenizer.apply_chat_template` result. The two templates differ: the Thinking one ends the generation prompt with `<think>\\n`.\n\n1xL4, nightly images, this diff overlaid on `nightly-5555a2d8` (`processor.py` is the same as current main):\n\n| build | `tokenizer=` | renderer `tokenizer.name_or_path` | `output.prompt` uses |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| nightly before #60825 (`fe4df3df`) | Thinking | `Qwen/Qwen3-VL-2B-Thinking` | Thinking template |\n| main (`5555a2d8`) | Thinking | `Qwen/Qwen3-VL-2B-Instruct` | Instruct template |\n| this PR | Thinking | Thinking snapshot path (see Details) | Thinking template |\n| main (`5555a2d8`) | not set | `Qwen/Qwen3-VL-2B-Instruct` | Instruct template |\n| this PR | not set | `Qwen/Qwen3-VL-2B-Instruct` | Instruct template |\n\nUnit test:\n\n```bash\npytest tests/transformers_utils/test_processor.py\n```\n\nNew test on main: `1 failed, 4 passed` (the tokenizer comes back named `Qwen/Qwen2-VL-2B-Instruct`). This PR: `5 passed`.\n\n`pre-commit` passes.\n\n## Details\n\nThe renamed tokenizer is the renderer's own object, so `resolve_chat_template` then loads the chat template from the model repo's processor. It also depends on ordering: with the same tokenizer object, `resolve_chat_template` returns the Thinking template before `get_processor(\"Qwen/Qwen3-VL-2B-Instruct\", tokenizer=tok)` runs and the Instruct template after it.\n\nThe fix only renames a tokenizer whose `name_or_path` is the snapshot `get_processor` just loaded from. A tokenizer from another repo keeps its name. In the default case the renderer's tokenizer still carries that same snapshot path, so it is renamed to the Hub ID as before.\n\nThe snapshot path in the table: `HfRenderer` makes a `copy.copy` of the tokenizer, and the cached tokenizer is rebuilt from the object loaded before `get_tokenizer` renamed it. Text-only models already report the snapshot path on main for this reason. It only changes the reported name, so I left it out of this PR.\n\nNot a duplicate: no open or closed PR or issue since #60825 mentions this. Searched for `name_or_path`, `get_processor tokenizer`, chat template with `--tokenizer`, and `60825`.\n\nAI assistance (Claude Code) was used to find the bug, write the change and run the validation above.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n",
